---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../../components/Byline.astro";
import DualDoors from "../../components/DualDoors.astro";
import LegalNote from "../../components/LegalNote.astro";
import ThreeNumbersTable from "../../components/ThreeNumbersTable.astro";
import IndexSeriesTable from "../../components/IndexSeriesTable.astro";
import { FEATURED } from "../../lib/briefs";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../../lib/links";
import { newsArticleNode } from "../../lib/schema";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";

const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Notes", path: "/brief" },
  { name: FEATURED.title, path: FEATURED.path },
];

const pageUrl = "https://fflintel.com/brief/three-numbers";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${FEATURED.title} | FFL Intel`}
  description={FEATURED.dek}
  path={FEATURED.path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.copilot}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  extraJsonLd={[
    newsArticleNode({
      url: pageUrl,
      headline: FEATURED.title,
      description: FEATURED.dek,
      datePublished: FEATURED.date,
      dateModified: FEATURED.date,
      image: `https://fflintel.com/${CANON_PATHS.copilot}`,
    }),
  ]}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p
      class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
    >
      {FEATURED.kicker} · Outdoor Wire, 13 April 2026
    </p>
    <h1
      class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-4xl text-balance mb-4"
    >
      {FEATURED.title}
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 mb-6">{FEATURED.dek}</p>
    <Byline dateLabel={FEATURED.dateLabel} read={FEATURED.read} />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.copilot)}
        alt={FEATURED.imageAlt}
        width="1792"
        height="1008"
        class="w-full object-cover object-top max-h-80"
      />
      <figcaption class="px-4 py-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-500">
        Betsy runs the numbers in the background. The point is the three
        signals — not a personality column.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        On 13 April 2026 Outdoor Wire published Gearfire’s RetailBI Firearm
        Sales Index for March. New guns sold at the same stores as last year:
        down 11.9 percent from March 2025, down 8.2 percent on the year. We
        read the same Outdoor Wire you do.
      </p>
      <p>
        One month is a blip. The whole year is the story. August 2025, the
        first monthly, was down 17 percent. November was the steep one: minus
        22.2. December put the 2025 year at minus 12.1. The annual later
        called it about 13 percent. January 2026 first came out at minus 6.2
        (Outdoor Wire, 9 February) and was revised to minus 5.7 on the
        February release (10 March), “to reflect the full same-store
        dataset.” February: minus 6.5, year-to-date minus 6.1. March got
        worse.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
      >
        The public prints
      </p>
      <IndexSeriesTable />
      <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-500">
        As of 13 August 2026 the last monthly on Outdoor Wire is still the
        April 13 release. We will not invent April through July.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <blockquote
        class="border-l-2 border-sky-500/60 pl-4 text-zinc-100 italic"
      >
        Figures are subject to revision as retailer data syncs are finalized.
        <span class="block mt-2 not-italic text-sm text-zinc-400"
          >— Outdoor Wire, 13 April 2026, RetailBI file</span
        >
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Believe that line. Their About says more than two thousand U.S. gun
        shops. It is still a sample. It is still last month. It is still only
        what sold.
      </p>
      <p>
        How they say they build it: sales from the register and the shop
        website; new guns only; only stores that reported this year and last
        year. A shop that opened or closed does not pretend to be demand. They
        built it because NICS was a bad stand-in for sales — permits, used
        guns, transfers, denials, two guns on one check. Kaleb Seymour put
        that on Outdoor Wire on 6 June 2024. They still say it on every Index
        release. They are right about that.
      </p>
      <p>
        NSSF, 23 February 2026, ran Gearfire’s free annual. 2025 new-gun sales
        at the same stores: down about 13 percent from 2024. Their 2026 guess:
        another seven to twelve percent down, “in the absence of a meaningful
        demand catalyst.” What the monthly number will not tell you: shotguns
        −16 percent, rifles −15.4, handguns −11.5 (still the biggest category).
        Versus 2019, new guns −10.5 percent. Year-end inventory −10.6 percent.
        Ammo units −12.4. Optics down on the year; red dots more than doubled
        versus 2019. Suppressors −19.3 after a strong 2024 — Gearfire says
        some buyers waited on the $200 tax stamp. Suppressor inventory up 21.8
        percent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Used guns, labeled as such: Guns.com, Outdoor Wire 12 March 2026,
        said local-dealer sales on their platform were up 46 percent in
        February and that they were beating that same 7–12 percent 2026 Index
        forecast. Used guns were about 31 percent of their mix. The Index is
        new guns only. Those sentences can all be true. Mixing them is how a
        forecast goes sideways.
      </p>
      <p>
        That sales number cannot see a search that came back empty. An empty
        search never becomes a unit sold. It still happened. We will not
        invent a Betsy percentage and park it next to −11.9 as if it were
        their Index. Different job. NICS is checks. RetailBI is what sold.
        Betsy BI is what people asked for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
      >
        Three numbers. Stop mixing these up.
      </p>
      <ThreeNumbersTable />
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        If you already read RetailBI, the side-by-side of the two ways of
        counting lives on GunSearchEngine:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.fortress} class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >RetailBI and Betsy, side by side</a
        >. If you run a shop, the site tool is{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.gsa} class={linkClass} {...ext}>GunSearchAgent</a>
        — a different product than this page.
      </p>
    </div>

    <DualDoors heading="What to do with the distinction" />

    <p class="mt-10">
      <LegalNote />
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
